            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Configuration in .NET</h2>
                    <p>.NET provides a flexible configuration system that supports multiple sources: JSON files, environment variables, command-line arguments, and more. This allows you to easily manage settings across different environments.</p>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Setting Up Configuration</h2>
                    <p>First, add the required packages:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash">dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration
dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Json
dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.EnvironmentVariables</code></pre>

                    <p>Create an <code>appsettings.json</code> file:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-json">{
  "AppSettings": {
    "ApplicationName": "My App",
    "MaxRetries": 3,
    "EnableFeatureX": true
  },
  "ConnectionStrings": {
    "DefaultConnection": "Server=localhost;Database=MyDb"
  },
  "Logging": {
    "LogLevel": "Information"
  }
}</code></pre>

                    <p>Make sure the file is copied to output:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-xml">&lt;ItemGroup&gt;
  &lt;None Update="appsettings.json"&gt;
    &lt;CopyToOutputDirectory&gt;PreserveNewest&lt;/CopyToOutputDirectory&gt;
  &lt;/None&gt;
&lt;/ItemGroup&gt;</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Reading Configuration</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">using Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration;

// Build configuration
IConfiguration config = new ConfigurationBuilder()
    .SetBasePath(Directory.GetCurrentDirectory())
    .AddJsonFile("appsettings.json", optional: false)
    .AddJsonFile($"appsettings.{Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable("DOTNET_ENVIRONMENT")}.json", optional: true)
    .AddEnvironmentVariables()
    .Build();

// Read values
string appName = config["AppSettings:ApplicationName"];
int maxRetries = config.GetValue&lt;int&gt;("AppSettings:MaxRetries");
string connectionString = config.GetConnectionString("DefaultConnection");

Console.WriteLine($"App: {appName}");
Console.WriteLine($"Max Retries: {maxRetries}");
Console.WriteLine($"Connection: {connectionString}");</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Environment-Specific Configuration</h2>
                    <p>Create environment-specific files that override base settings:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash">appsettings.json                 # Base settings
appsettings.Development.json     # Development overrides
appsettings.Production.json      # Production overrides</code></pre>

                    <p>Example <code>appsettings.Development.json</code>:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-json">{
  "ConnectionStrings": {
    "DefaultConnection": "Server=localhost;Database=MyDb_Dev"
  },
  "Logging": {
    "LogLevel": "Debug"
  }
}</code></pre>

                    <p>Set the environment via:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Windows PowerShell
$env:DOTNET_ENVIRONMENT = "Development"

# Linux/Mac
export DOTNET_ENVIRONMENT=Development

# In launchSettings.json for development
"environmentVariables": {
  "DOTNET_ENVIRONMENT": "Development"
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>The Options Pattern</h2>
                    <p>The Options pattern provides strongly-typed access to configuration sections:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash">dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.Options</code></pre>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">// Define options class
public class AppSettings
{
    public string ApplicationName { get; set; } = "";
    public int MaxRetries { get; set; }
    public bool EnableFeatureX { get; set; }
}

// Bind configuration to options
var appSettings = new AppSettings();
config.GetSection("AppSettings").Bind(appSettings);

// Or use Get&lt;T&gt;()
var settings = config.GetSection("AppSettings").Get&lt;AppSettings&gt;();

Console.WriteLine($"App: {settings?.ApplicationName}");
Console.WriteLine($"Feature X: {settings?.EnableFeatureX}");</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Using with Dependency Injection</h2>
                    <p>In hosted applications, use the Options pattern with DI:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Options;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// Register options
builder.Services.Configure&lt;AppSettings&gt;(
    builder.Configuration.GetSection("AppSettings"));

var host = builder.Build();

// Resolve options
var options = host.Services.GetRequiredService&lt;IOptions&lt;AppSettings&gt;&gt;();
Console.WriteLine($"App: {options.Value.ApplicationName}");</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Environment Variables</h2>
                    <p>Environment variables can override any configuration value:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Override AppSettings:MaxRetries
$env:AppSettings__MaxRetries = "5"

# Note: Use double underscore (__) for nested keys</code></pre>

                    <p>Configuration sources are layered—later sources override earlier ones:</p>

                    <div class="config-priority">
                        <div class="priority-item">1. appsettings.json (lowest priority)</div>
                        <div class="priority-item">2. appsettings.{Environment}.json</div>
                        <div class="priority-item">3. Environment variables</div>
                        <div class="priority-item">4. Command-line arguments (highest priority)</div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>User Secrets (Development)</h2>
                    <p>For sensitive data during development, use User Secrets:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Initialize user secrets
dotnet user-secrets init

# Set a secret
dotnet user-secrets set "ConnectionStrings:DefaultConnection" "Server=secret"

# List secrets
dotnet user-secrets list

# Remove a secret
dotnet user-secrets remove "ConnectionStrings:DefaultConnection"</code></pre>

                    <p>Add to configuration:</p>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">var config = new ConfigurationBuilder()
    .AddJsonFile("appsettings.json")
    .AddUserSecrets&lt;Program&gt;() // Only in Development
    .Build();</code></pre>

                    <div class="warning-box">
                        <div class="warning-box-header">
                            <span>⚠️ Important</span>
                        </div>
                        <p>User Secrets are stored outside your project folder and should only be used during development. For production, use environment variables or a secrets manager like Azure Key Vault.</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Command-Line Configuration</h2>
                    <pre><code class="language-bash">dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.CommandLine</code></pre>

                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">var config = new ConfigurationBuilder()
    .AddJsonFile("appsettings.json")
    .AddCommandLine(args)
    .Build();</code></pre>

                    <pre><code class="language-bash"># Override settings via command line
dotnet run -- --AppSettings:MaxRetries=10 --Logging:LogLevel=Debug</code></pre>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use <code>appsettings.json</code> for configuration with environment-specific overrides.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use the Options pattern for strongly-typed configuration access.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Use User Secrets for sensitive data during development.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Environment variables and command-line args override file-based config.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>
